    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP\u2019s May 20th slate, **Corbin Carroll** (83.49) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) anchor our lineup as the premier offensive plays. **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a strong platoon advantage against Tyler Mahle, and despite a slight drag from the catcher's pop time, his efficiency is boosted by his aggressive profile in the #2 hole. Meanwhile, **Mickey Moniak** thrives on a favorable park factor and a soft matchup against Jack Leiter, with the added benefit of a slow pitcher delivery that significantly increases his stolen base potential\u2014a key focus for our strategy. Similarly, **Tyler Soderstrom** (75.27) earns a high score by leveraging a favorable matchup against Jack Kochanowicz, where the SP skill projection suggests a high-scoring environment that favors his current platoon heat.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Seiya Suzuki** (52.60). While he posted a respectable score, he was ultimately nudged out by **Kyle Karros** (55.34) and our infield core. The optimizer penalized **Seiya Suzuki** due to a tough matchup against Kyle Harrison and unfavorable wind conditions, which dampened his power floor compared to the park-adjusted upside of **Kyle Karros** in the same game as **Mickey Moniak**. We are also monitoring **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who remains on the bench with a sub-40 score; his performance against left-handed pitching remains a liability that our model cannot justify starting today against Connelly Early.\n\nManagers should remain alert, as every slot in today's lineup is currently tagged as \"Lineup Pending.\" While we have projected positions based on historical data, late scratches or order shifts\u2014particularly for **Brice Turang**\u2014could impact the efficiency scores. Furthermore, while **Dalton Rushing** and **Gabriel Moreno** are slotted in, their sub-60 scores indicate they are barely clearing the threshold for active starts. We are proceeding with caution, prioritizing speed and 